Smart Parking Utilization System –
Abstract Project Documentation
1. Project Overview
Goal: Reduce parking search time and congestion using real-time occupancy
tracking and AI-based prediction.
Tech Stack: .NET 10 (C#), Angular, SQLite/MySQL, JWT authentication,
Tailwind CSS + DaisyUI.
2. Problem Statement

Drivers waste time finding empty slots.
No real-time visibility of parking utilization.
No prediction of future occupancy or peak hours.
Administrators lack automated alerts and utilization reports.
3. Features (Functional Scope)

User login with roles (Admin, Operator, Viewer).
Real-time dashboard (total/occupied/available slots, AI prediction).
Parking slot management (view, initialize, colour‑coded status).
Vehicle entry/exit tracking with transaction history.
AI prediction based on historical occupancy.
Alerts for high occupancy (>80% warning, >90% critical).
4. System Architecture (High‑Level)

Frontend: Angular – communicates via REST API.
Backend: ASP .NET Core Web API – handles business logic and
authentication.
Database: SQLite (development) / MS SQL (production).
Authentication: JWT – token stored in frontend, validated by middleware.
5. Design

Layered Architecture: Controllers → Services → DbContext → Database.
Dependency Injection: Services injected into controllers; DbContext per
request.
DTOs: Separate data contracts for API (hide internal fields).
Async/Await: All I/O operations non‑blocking.
6. Module Design

Auth Module: Login, JWT generation, password hashing (BCrypt).
Parking Module: Occupancy logic, slot management, entry/exit.
Prediction Module: Weighted regression (current + last 3h avg + historical
avg).
Reporting Module (partial): Daily/weekly trends, zone usage.
Frontend Modules: Login, Dashboard, Slots, Vehicle Entry, Reports.
7. API Design

RESTful endpoints: /api/auth/login, /api/parking/dashboard,
/api/parking/slots, /api/parking/entry, /api/parking/exit/{id},
/api/parking/init-slots.
Input/Output: JSON.
Authentication: Bearer token for all protected endpoints.
8. Data Flow
  1. User logs in → Backend validates credentials → Returns JWT.
  2. Frontend stores token → Includes it in every request header.
  3. Backend validates token via middleware → Controller processes request.
  4. Service uses Entity Framework to query/update SQLite database.
  5. Response returned as JSON → Frontend updates UI.
  6. Dashboard auto‑refreshes every 30 seconds.
9. Error Handling

Global exception middleware catches unhandled exceptions → returns
consistent error JSON.
Validation errors return 400 with details.
Authentication failures return 401.
Empty history in prediction falls back to current occupancy (defensive check).
Frontend shows user‑friendly messages for network or server errors.
10. Assumptions & Limitations
Assumptions:

Drivers are not direct users; operators/administrators manage the system.
Parking slots are fixed (no dynamic resizing).
Predictions use simple weighted formula until enough historical data is
collected.
Limitations:
No real‑time hardware sensors (manual entry/exit).
No email/SMS alerts (only dashboard notifications).
Prediction accuracy improves over time as occupancy history grows.
